# {{.DisplayName}}

{{.Description}}

This project was created with Bridra {{.FrameworkVersion}} Project Template
v{{.TemplateVersion}} for: {{range $index, $platform := .Platforms}}{{if $index}}, {{end}}`{{$platform}}`{{end}}. It keeps Flutter
responsible for the cross-platform interface and Go responsible for middleware,
request validation, controllers, services, models, and transports.

## Flutter UI

The starter separates application-owned UI into:

- `lib/app/`: composition, local navigation and appearance selection.
- `lib/ui/`: light/dark themes, collapsible adaptive sidebar/bar navigation and shared widgets.
- `lib/features/`: Workspace, Runtime, Guide and Settings views with presentation state.
- `lib/api/`: the typed backend gateway and generated RPC contracts.

Workspace contains a working greeting request/response flow. Runtime displays
the same connection's health, Guide provides copyable development commands, and
Settings offers System/Light/Dark appearance controls.
Navigation, sidebar collapse and appearance changes retain the connection and
request draft. The top toolbar places Back, Forward and animated sidebar
controls after the application name, followed by the current page title on the
same row. The header shares the sidebar background. The toggle stays available
at every size: it expands the icon sidebar on desktops and opens an overlay
below the header in narrow windows. Section history remains in memory.
Settings opens inside the main content card and participates in Back/Forward
history. The sidebar keeps it at the bottom, with an icon and label when expanded
and a tooltip when compact. Narrow windows also expose Settings in bottom
navigation and the overlay sidebar. Backend health details remain in Runtime;
there is no toolbar/sidebar status badge. Appearance defaults to System and
follows device changes; explicit appearance and sidebar choices last for the
current process.
Add application-owned persistence or routing when needed.

Copy confirmations appear as a floating toast below the toolbar and dismiss
automatically after three seconds. Feature views can use
`AppToastHost.show(context, message)` for the same nonblocking feedback; repeated
messages replace the current toast, and reduced motion disables its animation.

Extend this UI inside your project. Dependency upgrades do not replace it;
template rendering also preserves existing UI and starter test files.

## Development

```bash
fvm install
fvm flutter pub get
make doctor
make verify
make run
```

{{if hasPlatform "linux"}}The starter's Linux CJK fallback uses installed `Noto Sans CJK` or
`Droid Sans Fallback` fonts. On Ubuntu, install `fonts-noto-cjk` if the app needs
Chinese, Japanese, or Korean text. Some ARM64 Flutter engines cannot discover
fallback fonts through Fontconfig automatically, so the starter explicitly
lists these families in `ThemeData.fontFamilyFallback`. Applications that need
portable typography should bundle appropriately licensed fonts.

{{end}}
{{if hasPlatform "macos"}}The macOS starter draws its interface into a transparent title bar, retaining
native window controls and dragging. The toolbar places the app name and
navigation controls beside the native buttons. Window styling lives in
`macos/Runner/MainFlutterWindow.swift`.

On macOS, `make macos-native-test` verifies the native lifecycle bridge used to
terminate an acknowledged secondary application instance before it enters
`runApp`, plus the bookmark creator used for native resource grants.

`make macos-sandbox-smoke` signs two temporary sandboxed harness apps and uses
an outside-container folder to prove raw-path denial, ephemeral bookmark grant
success, and denial again after release. It removes the temporary folder and
apps after the gate and reuses two fixed bundle identifiers to avoid per-run
macOS container growth.

For a sandboxed app that must give the Go Sidecar access to a user-selected
resource, build the Sidecar with native mode and application-owned entitlements.
Create the bookmark with `MacOSResourceBookmarks.create`, grant it through
`SidecarClient.grantResource`, pass only `capability.value` to the
application-owned RPC request, and call `releaseResource` when finished. Treat
bookmark bytes and capability values as authority-bearing secrets: never log or
persist them. Persistent bookmark storage and re-grant policy remain owned by
the application.

{{end}}
`make run` builds the Go sidecar, exposes its path to Flutter, and starts the
current Flutter target. Desktop uses the Sidecar by default; selected Mobile and
Web targets use the HTTP backend:

```bash
make backend-serve
fvm flutter run -d chrome \
  --dart-define=BRIDRA_BACKEND_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080/rpc \
  --dart-define=BRIDRA_BACKEND_TOKEN=dev-token
```

The Bridra CLI can supervise the same development session through the project's
versioned framework dependency:

```bash
cd backend
go run {{.BridraGoModule}}/cmd/bridra dev --root ..
go run {{.BridraGoModule}}/cmd/bridra dev --root .. --device chrome
```

Mobile and custom devices require `--listen 0.0.0.0:<port>` plus an explicit
`--backend-url` reachable from the device.

{{if hasPlatform "android"}}With an Android Emulator already running, exercise the generated app and a real
temporary Go backend without Android hardware:

```bash
make android-emulator-smoke
```

The gate uses Android's `10.0.2.2` host alias and verifies Health, Greeting,
ordered Streaming/Progress, interrupted and resumed managed downloads/uploads,
a backend outage, and the complete flow again after reconnect. Debug and Profile
manifests allow local cleartext traffic; Release keeps Android's secure default.

{{end}}{{if hasPlatform "ios"}}On macOS with full Xcode, exercise the generated iOS app and real Go HTTP
backend in a Simulator or on a trusted physical iPhone:

```bash
make ios-simulator-smoke
make ios-device-smoke
```

The Simulator target verifies Health, Greeting, ordered Streaming/Progress, and
managed file transfers through the running iOS app. It interrupts a download
after 32 KiB and requires a byte-range resume while preserving known size,
SHA-256, and content. It also interrupts an upload at the same offset, requires
resume from that exact byte, and has Go consume and re-hash the completed upload.
Its Flutter phase stops after nine minutes total or four minutes without output.
It also delays only `simctl launch` by five seconds to avoid Flutter 3.44's
upstream Simulator log-reader startup race; override that bounded barrier with
`BRIDRA_IOS_SIMULATOR_LAUNCH_DELAY_SECONDS`.
Set `BRIDRA_IOS_SIMULATOR_DIAGNOSTICS_DIR` to preserve bounded failure evidence.
It is suitable for hosted macOS CI.

The device target selects the only available iPhone unless `DEVICE=<id>` is
provided, avoids occupied local ports, verifies Health and Greeting, forces a
backend outage and verifies reconnect, checks ordered Streaming and Progress
plus interrupted/resumed managed downloads and uploads before and after recovery,
and proves that a signed Profile app cold-launches twice without Flutter tooling.
Configure an Apple Development Team before running it and accept the one-time
Local Network prompt for a new bundle identifier; signing remains
application-owned.

For an opt-in device-local backend, run `make ios-embedded-core-build`.
This builds the application package at `backend/mobilebridge` into the ignored
`ios/Frameworks/AppCore.xcframework`; it does not bind Bridra's reference app.
Add that XCFramework and `ios/Runner/BridraEmbeddedRuntime.swift` to the Runner
target, then call `installBridraEmbeddedRuntime(token:)` once from the
application-owned `AppDelegate`. Construct `EmbeddedRpcClient` with the same
token and `MethodChannelEmbeddedRpcBridge` in Dart. Bridra never edits the Xcode
project or chooses the app's runtime automatically, so signing, capabilities,
persistence, and rollback remain explicit application decisions.

The generated Go wrapper, Swift adapter, and isolated gomobile module are
application-owned create-time seeds. Re-rendering the Project Template leaves
existing copies unchanged. Unary calls, pull-backed server streams, exact
request cancellation, managed file transfer, and bounded close are supported.
File contents use bounded typed-byte chunks with download resume, upload offset
recovery, declared-size checks, and SHA-256 verification; they are not embedded
inside JSON RPC. The generated runtime adapter also exposes `grantResourcePath`
and `releaseResource`: after an application-owned `UIDocumentPicker` returns a
directory URL, call `BridraFlutterPlugin.grantSecurityScopedResource` on the
main thread and pass only its opaque capability to Dart／RPC. Detach before
calling `releaseSecurityScopedResource`; runtime close releases any remaining
scope. Picker UI, background interruption, cold launch, and physical-device
acceptance remain separate application gates.

{{end}}
## Builds

Build selected desktop Sidecar bundles or mobile/Web HTTP artifacts through the same local
Bridra CLI dependency:

```bash
{{if hasPlatform "macos"}}make macos-build
{{end}}{{if hasPlatform "linux"}}make linux-build
{{end}}{{if hasPlatform "windows"}}make windows-build
{{end}}{{if hasPlatform "android"}}make android-build BACKEND_URL=https://api.example.com/rpc
{{end}}{{if hasPlatform "ios"}}make ios-build BACKEND_URL=https://api.example.com/rpc
{{end}}{{if hasPlatform "web"}}make web-build \
  BACKEND_URL=https://api.example.com/rpc \
  BACKEND_TOKEN=replace-me
{{end}}
```

Set `BUILD_MODE=debug`, `profile`, or `release`. Profile and release HTTP builds
require an HTTPS `/rpc` endpoint and token. Each successful build writes a
token-free SHA-256 manifest under `build/bridra/`. iOS output is unsigned;
signing, notarization, installers, and store submission are separate release
steps.

## Contract generation

`schema/bridra.json` is the RPC source of truth. The project also owns
`schema/bridra.baseline.json`, an immutable snapshot of the reviewed deployed
contract:

```bash
make generate
make schema-check
make codegen-check
```

`make verify` runs the schema check automatically. A breaking wire change fails
unless the current application `protocolVersion` is greater than the baseline.
Neither `make generate` nor framework upgrades replace the baseline. Before the
first release, explicitly accept a reviewed initial contract from the project
root with `bridra schema baseline --update`. This validates and copies the current
schema; review the baseline diff before committing. Once peers are deployed,
update it only after the new contract has been reviewed and deployed.

To compare against a different release or deployment explicitly:

```bash
cd backend
go run {{.BridraGoModule}}/cmd/bridra schema check \
  --against path/to/released-schema.json \
  --schema ../schema/bridra.json
```

Use `--json` for a machine-readable CI report.

Set `"stream": true` on a schema method to generate a typed
`Stream<RpcStreamEvent<ResultType>>`; use `framework.ProduceStream` in its Go
Controller.

Use a `"type": "file"` field for large input or output. Stage output through
`framework.FileTransferStoreKey`, then consume its generated `RpcFileReference`
with `BackendGateway.download`. Create input with `RpcFileUpload`, call
`BackendGateway.upload`, and consume it in Go with
`FileTransferStore.ConsumeUpload`. Desktop uses managed temporary files; HTTP
uses short-lived capabilities with byte-range retry. The opt-in iOS Embedded
Core uses opaque download handles and bounded MethodChannel typed-byte chunks;
uploads recover the Go-confirmed offset after an interrupted response. All
paths verify size and SHA-256.

